Remove the unreachable SSE dispatch that breaks universal builds.

A universal Mach-O build compiles every slice from one configure run,
but configure probes the host once. On an Apple Silicon runner the
"working SSE intrinsics" test compiles _mm_sfence(), fails, and leaves
HAVE_XMMINTRIN_H undefined -- so xmm_quantize_sub.c never compiles
init_xrpow_core_sse. The x86_64 slice still links against it:

    Undefined symbols for architecture x86_64:
      "_init_xrpow_core_sse"

Two earlier approaches do not work. Seeding ac_cv_header_xmmintrin_h is
ignored, because the check is a hand-rolled AC_COMPILE_IFELSE that
assigns the variable unconditionally rather than an AC_CHECK_HEADER that
consults the cache. Gating on __SSE__ does not help either: it is not
defined while compiling the x86_64 slice here.

The dispatch being removed is already dead on this build path. It is
guarded by gfc->CPU_features.SSE, and has_SSE() in util.c returns 0
whenever HAVE_NASM is undefined and neither _M_X64 nor MIN_ARCH_SSE is
defined -- which is exactly the autotools configuration. The branch can
never be taken, so deleting it changes no behaviour and removes the only
reference that survives when the definition does not.

The MIN_ARCH_SSE block below it is left alone: that macro is defined
only by LAME's MSVC project files, so it is already inert here.

--- a/libmp3lame/quantize.c
+++ b/libmp3lame/quantize.c
@@ -94,10 +94,6 @@
 {
     gfc->init_xrpow_core = init_xrpow_core_c;
 
-#if defined(HAVE_XMMINTRIN_H)
-    if (gfc->CPU_features.SSE)
-        gfc->init_xrpow_core = init_xrpow_core_sse;
-#endif
 #ifndef HAVE_NASM
 #ifdef MIN_ARCH_SSE
     gfc->init_xrpow_core = init_xrpow_core_sse;
